# LM configuration for tools/kpwiki — copy to .env and source it by hand.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && $EDITOR .env && set -a && . ./.env && set +a
#
# Nothing in this repository reads a dotenv file. The LM layer takes its keys
# straight from the environment (tools/kpwiki/lm.py), so this file is a record
# of the configuration, not a loader. `.env` itself is git-ignored.

# --- keys ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Each provider prefix reads the key named in lm.py → PROVIDER_KEY_ENV.
# Setting either one makes KP_LM_BACKEND=auto resolve to the `api` backend;
# with neither, `auto` falls back to the `claude` CLI on a Claude Code
# subscription, which ignores every KP_LM_* variable below.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-...
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# --- backend ------------------------------------------------------------
# `auto` is enough once a key is set. Pin it to `api` to be explicit, or to
# `claude-cli` to force the subscription path regardless of keys.
KP_LM_BACKEND=api

# --- roles --------------------------------------------------------------
# The three roles resolve independently, so mixing providers is supported and
# is the cheap configuration. Note the doubled vendor segment: LiteLLM needs
# `openrouter/<vendor>/<model>`, and the Free Models Router's own id is
# `openrouter/free` — hence `openrouter/openrouter/free`.

# All three roles are DEFAULT (Anthropic) by author decision, 2026-09-16: the
# DSPy programs keep a provider whose typed-output behaviour is known, and
# OpenRouter is used only where no typed schema is at stake.
#
# The measurements that would support switching are kept rather than discarded,
# so the decision can be revisited without re-running the sweep
# (Plan/quality/lm-bench_2026-09-16.md):
#
#   worker      openrouter/openrouter/free          100% schema-valid, score 1.00, 28.7 s, free
#   worker      openrouter/nex-agi/nex-n2.5-pro:free 100% schema-valid, score 1.00, 35.2 s, free
#   task        openrouter/inclusionai/ling-3.0-flash 100% schema-valid, score 1.00, 24.3 s
#
# Against those numbers stands one observation the score could not see: the
# router returned correct German claims and scored 1.00 while its triage summary
# came back in English. Canon prose is German and is never translated, so
# language drift is a defect the metric does not currently catch.
#
# KP_LM_TASK=anthropic/claude-opus-5
# KP_LM_WORKER=anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5
# KP_LM_REFLECTION=anthropic/claude-opus-5
#
# reflection should not take a router whatever it scores: GEPA would tune
# instructions for a model that will not necessarily serve the next request.

# --- lit-critic on OpenRouter (the one non-DSPy LLM surface) -------------
# scripts/lit_critic_gate.py is the only LLM step in this repo that is not a
# DSPy program: it reviews prose in free text, with no typed schema and no
# closed enum, which is exactly where a cheaper provider carries no schema risk.
#
# lit-critic knows two providers (anthropic, openai) and passes no base_url,
# but the OpenAI SDK reads OPENAI_BASE_URL from the environment, so OpenRouter
# works through it with no patch to the vendored tool:
#
#   OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
#   OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-...          # the OpenRouter key goes here
#
# Verified live against .lit-critic-src/.venv (openai SDK 3.14.1).
#
# One caveat before using the free router here. It routes to a different model
# per call, and some of those are reasoning models whose chain of thought is
# billed against max_tokens. Measured on three consecutive identical calls:
#
#   max_tokens=400    -> content None, finish_reason=length, 400 reasoning tokens
#   max_tokens=4096   -> correct German,  370 reasoning tokens
#   max_tokens=16000  -> correct German,   25 reasoning tokens
#
# lit-critic hardcodes max_tokens 4096 for its openai models
# (.lit-critic-src/orchestrator/runtime/config.py). That was sufficient in the
# measured run, but the reasoning overhead is not bounded and a longer-reasoning
# model can return empty content with no error. A fixed cheap model is the safer
# choice here than the router.

# --- cache --------------------------------------------------------------
# DSPy keys its cache on the prompt, not on the model that answered. With a
# router that hides per-call variance after the first hit, so clear the cache
# when measuring reliability rather than throughput.
# DSPY_CACHEDIR=.cache/dspy
